# .pii-allow — reviewed exemptions for the PII/secret scanner.
#
# One regex per line, matched against the WHOLE LINE. Every entry is a value that
# was inspected and found synthetic. Deliberately small and specific: it exempts
# VALUES, never files or paths, so a real secret added to an exempted file is
# still caught.
#
# Why this exists: `pii_scan.py --history` returned findings that were all
# fixtures in the CI-diagnostician's own test — the detector's header→token
# mapping. A scan that can never come back clean trains everyone to ignore it,
# which is the alarm-fatigue failure `references/notification-taxonomy.md` exists
# to prevent. Exempting fixtures is what makes the next real finding mean
# something.
#
# Reviewed 2026-07-26. Verified with a negative control: a realistic key body is
# still BLOCKed with these entries in place. Re-run that check before adding an
# entry — an allowlist is the one place where being lazy silently disables the
# control.
#
# NOT used here on purpose: the file-level `pii-scan: ignore-file` marker. These
# are ordinary test files that happen to contain fixtures, not dedicated corpora,
# so a whole-file exemption would blind the scanner to a real secret landing in
# them later.

# AWS's own documented example access key ID, from their public docs. The
# canonical "not a real key" value; it cannot authenticate to anything.
AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE

# A PEM header as a COMPLETE quoted string — opening quote, header, closing quote
# — with no key body anywhere on the line. Safe because a header alone is not key
# material: the quotes bound it, so no base64 body can be present.
["']-----BEGIN [A-Z ]+PRIVATE KEY-----["']
